\label{ch:linear-types}

Linear type systems track the usage of values: a linear value must be used exactly once, while an affine value may be used at most once. These disciplines, rooted in linear logic~\cite{girard1987}, provide static guarantees about resource usage---memory, file handles, locks, and other finite resources. This chapter develops the theory of linear and affine types, their connection to linear logic, and their application in modern programming languages, culminating in Rust's ownership system.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 48.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Linear Logic and Linear Types}\label{sec:linear-logic}

Linear logic, introduced by Girard~\cite{girard1987}, is a logic in which every hypothesis must be used exactly once. This stands in contrast to classical and intuitionistic logic, where hypotheses can be used any number of times (contraction) or discarded (weakening).

\subsection{The Resources Interpretation}\label{sec:resources-interpretation}

In linear logic, propositions are interpreted as \emph{resources}: consuming a resource to prove a proposition means that resource is no longer available. The linear implication $A \multimap B$ means ``given one unit of $A$, I can produce one unit of $B$,'' and the premise $A$ is consumed in the process.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Structural rules and their resource interpretation}\label{tab:structural-rules}
\begin{tabular}{lp{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Rule} & \textbf{Logic} & \textbf{Resource Interpretation} \\
\hline
Weakening & $\Gamma \vdash A \implies \Gamma, B \vdash A$ & Discarding a resource \\
Contraction & $\Gamma, A, A \vdash B \implies \Gamma, A \vdash B$ & Duplicating a resource \\
Exchange & $\Gamma, A, B \vdash C \implies \Gamma, B, A \vdash C$ & Reordering resources \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Linear logic retains exchange but drops weakening and contraction: resources cannot be discarded or duplicated.

\subsection{Linear Type System}\label{sec:linear-type-system}

\begin{grammar}{Linear Lambda Calculus}
  \production{\tau}{\alpha \mid \tau_1 \multimap \tau_2 \mid \tau_1 \otimes \tau_2 \mid \mathbf{1} \mid \tau_1 \oplus \tau_2 \mid \mathbf{0}}
\end{grammar}

The linear function type $\tau_1 \multimap \tau_2$ represents a function that consumes exactly one argument of type $\tau_1$ and produces a result of type $\tau_2$. The tensor product $\tau_1 \otimes \tau_2$ represents a pair where both components must be used. The unit $\mathbf{1}$ is the type with a single value that must be used (trivially).

\begin{typerules}{Linear Lambda Calculus}
  \typeaxiom{Var}{x : \tau \vdash x : \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs}{\Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e : \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash \lambda x.\, e : \tau_1 \multimap \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App}{\Gamma_1 \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \multimap \tau_2 \quad \Gamma_2 \vdash e_2 : \tau_1}
            {\Gamma_1, \Gamma_2 \vdash e_1\; e_2 : \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Tensor-Intro}{\Gamma_1 \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \quad \Gamma_2 \vdash e_2 : \tau_2}
            {\Gamma_1, \Gamma_2 \vdash (e_1, e_2) : \tau_1 \otimes \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Tensor-Elim}{\Gamma_1 \vdash e : \tau_1 \otimes \tau_2 \quad \Gamma_2, x : \tau_1, y : \tau_2 \vdash e' : \tau}
            {\Gamma_1, \Gamma_2 \vdash \mathtt{let}\; (x, y) = e\; \mathtt{in}\; e' : \tau}
\end{typerules}

The key constraint is the \emph{splitting} of the environment: in the App rule, $\Gamma_1$ and $\Gamma_2$ must be a \emph{partition} of the available variables. Each variable is used in exactly one sub-expression.

\subsection{Linearity Enforcement}\label{sec:linearity-enforcement}

\begin{lemma}[Linearity]\label{lem:linearity}
In a well-typed linear program, every variable bound by a lambda or let is used exactly once in its scope.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the typing derivation. The App and Tensor-Intro rules split the environment, ensuring each variable appears in exactly one premise. The Abs rule binds a variable that must be used in the body. \qed
\end{proof}

