\section{Resource Tracking}\label{sec:resource-tracking}

Linear types provide static guarantees about resource usage. The most important application is ensuring that resources are properly acquired and released.

\subsection{File Handles}\label{sec:file-handles}

\begin{example}[Linear file handles]\label{ex:linear-files}
\begin{verbatim}
  open  : String -> FileHandle
  read  : FileHandle -o String * FileHandle
  close : FileHandle -o Unit
\end{verbatim}
The type of $\mathtt{read}$ says: given a file handle, produce a string and the same file handle (which can be used again). The type of $\mathtt{close}$ says: given a file handle, consume it and produce nothing. Since the file handle is linear, it must be closed exactly once---it cannot be discarded (no weakening) or used after closing (no contraction).
\end{example}

\subsection{Locks and Mutexes}\label{sec:locks}

\begin{example}[Linear locks]\label{ex:linear-locks}
\begin{verbatim}
  acquire : Mutex -o Lock
  release : Lock -o Mutex
  withLock : Lock -o (Lock * a) -o a  (* must return the lock *)
\end{verbatim}
A lock must be released after use. The type of $\mathtt{withLock}$ ensures that the lock is returned, so it can be released.
\end{example}

\subsection{Uniqueness Types}\label{sec:uniqueness-types}

Clean~\cite{clean1990} uses \emph{uniqueness types} to track unique references to mutable data. A unique reference guarantees exclusive access: no other reference to the same data exists.

\begin{verbatim}
  updateArray : *Array a -> Int -> a -> *Array a
\end{verbatim}

The asterisk denotes a unique reference. The function can mutate the array in place because it has exclusive access, and it returns a new unique reference to the (mutated) array.

