\section{Affine Types and Disposal}\label{sec:affine-types}

Affine types relax linearity by allowing values to be used \emph{at most once} rather than \emph{exactly once}. This corresponds to linear logic with weakening but without contraction.

\subsection{Affine Lambda Calculus}\label{sec:affine-calculus}

The affine lambda calculus is identical to the linear lambda calculus, except that the environment splitting rule is relaxed:

\[
  \typerule{Affine-App}{\Gamma_1 \vdash e_1 : \tau_1 \multimap \tau_2 \quad \Gamma_2 \vdash e_2 : \tau_1}
            {\Gamma_1 \uplus \Gamma_2 \vdash e_1\; e_2 : \tau_2}
\]

where $\uplus$ allows $\Gamma_1$ and $\Gamma_2$ to be \emph{disjoint subsets} of the available variables (some variables may be unused).

\subsection{Disposal}\label{sec:disposal}

Affine types require a mechanism for \emph{disposal}: if a value is not used, it must be explicitly or implicitly discarded. For types that can be safely discarded (e.g., integers, booleans), the compiler inserts an implicit drop. For types that require cleanup (e.g., file handles), the programmer must explicitly dispose of them.

\begin{definition}[Disposable Types]\label{def:disposable}
A type $\tau$ is \emph{disposable} (or \emph{droppable}) if there exists a function $\mathtt{drop} : \tau \multimap \mathbf{1}$. Base types like $\mathrm{int}$ and $\mathrm{bool}$ are disposable; resource types like $\mathrm{FileHandle}$ are not.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Affine Types in Practice}\label{sec:affine-practice}

Rust's ownership system is essentially an affine type system:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Values are \emph{moved} (used at most once) by default.
  \item Types that implement \texttt{Copy} can be implicitly duplicated (restoring contraction for those types).
  \item Types that implement \texttt{Drop} have a destructor called when they go out of scope (implicit disposal).
\end{itemize}

