\section{Bounded Types and Capabilities}\label{sec:bounded-types}

Bounded linear types allow a resource to be used a bounded number of times, generalizing both linear (exactly once) and affine (at most once) types.

\subsection{Usage Annotations}\label{sec:usage-annotations}

\begin{definition}[Usage Annotation]\label{def:usage}
A \emph{usage annotation} is an element of a lattice of usages. The simplest lattice is:
\[
  0 \leq 1 \leq \omega
\]
where $0$ means ``unused,'' $1$ means ``used once,'' and $\omega$ means ``used any number of times.''
\end{definition}

Types can be annotated with usage information: $\tau^{\pi}$ means ``a value of type $\tau$ with usage $\pi$.'' The function type becomes:
\[
  \tau_1^{\pi_1} \to^{\pi} \tau_2^{\pi_2}
\]
meaning ``a function that uses its argument with usage $\pi_1$ and produces a result with usage $\pi_2$, and the function itself has usage $\pi$.''

\subsection{Capabilities}\label{sec:capabilities}

The \emph{capability calculus} extends linear types with a notion of \emph{capabilities}: permissions to perform certain operations.

\begin{example}[Capabilities for memory]\label{ex:capabilities}
\begin{verbatim}
  ReadCap   : permission to read from a memory location
  WriteCap  : permission to write to a memory location
  AllocCap  : permission to allocate new memory
  FreeCap   : permission to free allocated memory
\end{verbatim}
A pointer with $\mathrm{ReadCap}$ can be read but not written. A pointer with both $\mathrm{ReadCap}$ and $\mathrm{WriteCap}$ is a mutable reference. Capabilities are linear: they must be passed explicitly and cannot be forged.
\end{example}

