\section{Applications: File Handles, Unique Pointers}\label{sec:applications}

\subsection{File Handles Revisited}\label{sec:files-revisited}

A complete linear type system for file handles ensures:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Every opened file is eventually closed.
  \item No file is used after being closed.
  \item No file is closed more than once.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  processFile : String -> (FileHandle -o a * FileHandle) -o a
  processFile path f =
    let fh = open path
    let (result, fh') = f fh
    close fh'
    result
\end{verbatim}

The function $\mathtt{processFile}$ opens a file, passes the handle to the user function $f$, and ensures the handle is closed afterward. The linear type of $f$ guarantees that the handle is returned (not discarded).

\subsection{Unique Pointers}\label{sec:unique-pointers}

A unique pointer is a pointer that is the sole reference to a block of memory. It can be used for:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{In-place mutation.} Since the pointer is unique, the memory can be mutated without affecting other references.
  \item \textbf{Deterministic deallocation.} When the unique pointer goes out of scope, the memory is freed.
  \item \textbf{Transfer of ownership.} Moving a unique pointer transfers ownership of the memory.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Unique pointers in Clean]\label{ex:clean-unique}
\begin{verbatim}
  sortArray : *Array Int -> *Array Int
  sortArray arr = sortInPlace arr  -- mutates in place, safe because unique
\end{verbatim}
The unique pointer guarantees that no other code holds a reference to the array, so in-place sorting is safe.
\end{example}

