\section{Connection to Rust's Ownership System}\label{sec:rust-ownership}

Rust's ownership system is the most successful application of linear/affine types in a mainstream programming language. It provides memory safety without garbage collection by tracking ownership and borrowing at compile time.

\subsection{Ownership and Moves}\label{sec:ownership-moves}

In Rust, every value has a single \emph{owner}. When a value is assigned to another variable or passed to a function, ownership is \emph{moved}:

\begin{verbatim}
  let s1 = String::from("hello");
  let s2 = s1;  // s1 is moved to s2; s1 is no longer valid
  // println!("{}", s1);  // ERROR: s1 has been moved
\end{verbatim}

This is the affine discipline: each value is used at most once (by move). Types that implement \texttt{Copy} (like integers) are implicitly cloned instead of moved, restoring the ability to use them multiple times.

\subsection{Borrowing}\label{sec:rust-borrowing}

Rust allows \emph{borrowing}: creating a reference to a value without transferring ownership. The borrow checker enforces:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Shared borrows} (\texttt{\&T}): multiple readers, no writers.
  \item \textbf{Exclusive borrows} (\texttt{\&mut T}): one writer, no readers.
  \item \textbf{Lifetimes}: borrows cannot outlive the owner.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
  let r1 = &v;       // shared borrow
  let r2 = &v;       // another shared borrow (OK)
  // let r3 = &mut v; // ERROR: cannot borrow as mutable while shared borrows exist
  println!("{} {}", r1[0], r2[0]);
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{The Type-Theoretic View}\label{sec:rust-type-theory}

Rust's type system can be understood as a combination of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Affine types.} Values are moved (used at most once) by default.
  \item \textbf{Borrow types.} References are linear (must be used) and carry lifetime annotations.
  \item \textbf{Substructural regions.} Lifetimes define regions of memory, and references are indexed by regions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
Rust's ownership system demonstrates that linear/affine types can be made practical for everyday programming. The key insight is that most programs are ``mostly affine'': values are used once (moved) or many times (copied), and the interesting cases involve borrowing. The borrow checker enforces a discipline that is essentially a form of linear typing for references, ensuring that mutable and immutable accesses are properly separated.
\end{insight}

