\begin{historical}
Linear logic was introduced by Girard~\cite{girard1987} as a refinement of classical and intuitionistic logic that tracks resource usage. The application to programming languages was pioneered by Wadler~\cite{wadler1990} and Abramsky~\cite{abramsky1993}, who showed that linear types can enforce protocols and prevent resource leaks. The Clean language~\cite{clean1990} was the first to use uniqueness types for efficient I/O in a pure functional language. Rust's ownership system, developed by Graydon Hoare and the Rust team, brought linear/affine types to a systems programming audience, demonstrating that these ideas can scale to large, performance-critical codebases.
\end{historical}

