\section{Advanced Resource Tracking and Rust Connections}\label{sec:resource-advanced}

\subsection{Usage Protocols}\label{sec:usage-protocols}

Linear types can be extended to track \emph{usage protocols}: sequences of operations that must be performed on a resource.

\begin{definition}[Usage Protocol]\label{def:usage-protocol}
A \emph{usage protocol} is a regular language over operations that describes the valid sequences of operations on a resource. For example, the protocol for a file handle is:
\[
  \mathrm{open} \cdot (\mathrm{read} + \mathrm{write})^* \cdot \mathrm{close}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Protocol for database connections]\label{ex:db-protocol}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Protocol: connect -> (query -> result)* -> disconnect
  type DbConn = {
    connect   : () -o QueryPhase
    query     : QueryPhase -o Result * QueryPhase
    disconnect : QueryPhase -o ()
  }
\end{verbatim}
The linear type ensures that a database connection is properly opened, used for zero or more queries, and then closed.
\end{example}

\subsection{Session Types as Linear Types}\label{sec:session-as-linear}

Session types (see \cref{ch:session-types}) are a special case of linear types applied to communication channels. The linearity of channels ensures that each message is sent and received exactly once.

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Session type as linear type:
  send : !String.end -o String -o end
  recv : ?String.S -o (String -o S)
  -- The channel is consumed by each operation
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Rust's Borrow Checker in Detail}\label{sec:rust-borrow-detail}

Rust's borrow checker implements a form of linear/affine typing with additional features for shared and exclusive access.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Rust's type system as affine types:
  // - Values are moved (affine: used at most once)
  // - &T is a shared borrow (can be duplicated: contraction allowed)
  // - &mut T is an exclusive borrow (linear: used exactly once)
  // - Lifetimes ensure borrows don't outlive the owner

  fn process(data: &mut Vec<i32>) {
      data.push(42);  // exclusive access: no other references
  }

  fn read_all(data: &Vec<i32>) -> i32 {
      data.iter().sum()  // shared access: multiple readers allowed
  }
\end{verbatim}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Mapping between linear type concepts and Rust features}\label{tab:rust-linear}
\begin{tabular}{p{4cm}p{4cm}p{4cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Linear Type Concept} & \textbf{Rust Feature} & \textbf{Enforcement} \\
\hline
Linear usage & Move semantics & Compile-time check \\
Affine (at most once) & Drop on scope exit & Implicit destructor \\
Contraction forbidden & No implicit copy & Move on assignment \\
Weakening allowed & Drop trait & Implicit drop \\
Tensor product & Tuple of owned values & Both must be used \\
Linear implication & Function with owned args & Args consumed \\
Unique reference & \texttt{\&mut T} & Exclusive access \\
Shared reference & \texttt{\&T} & Multiple readers \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Lifetime Annotations as Region Types}\label{sec:lifetime-regions}

Rust's lifetime annotations can be understood as \emph{region types} in the sense of linear type theory:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Rust with explicit lifetimes:
  fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {
      if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }
  }

  // As region types:
  longest : forall 'a. (String @ 'a, String @ 'a) -> String @ 'a
  -- The result lives in the intersection of the input lifetimes
\end{verbatim}

\begin{definition}[Region Type]\label{def:region-type}
A \emph{region type} $\tau @ \rho$ pairs a value type $\tau$ with a region $\rho$ that describes where the value is valid. The region $\rho$ is a lifetime annotation that constrains how long the value can be borrowed.
\end{definition}

\subsection{NLL: Non-Lexical Lifetimes}\label{sec:nll}

Rust's original borrow checker used \emph{lexical lifetimes}: a borrow was valid until the end of its enclosing scope. The \emph{Non-Lexical Lifetimes} (NLL) system refines this by computing the precise region where a borrow is actually used.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before NLL (lexical):
  let mut data = vec![1, 2, 3];
  let r = &data[0];   // borrow starts
  println!("{}", r);   // last use of r
  data.push(4);        // ERROR: cannot borrow as mutable while r is alive
  // (r's lexical scope extends to end of block)

  // After NLL:
  let mut data = vec![1, 2, 3];
  let r = &data[0];   // borrow starts
  println!("{}", r);   // last use of r -> borrow ends here
  data.push(4);        // OK: borrow has ended
\end{verbatim}

