\subsection{Linear Haskell}\label{sec:linear-haskell}

Linear Haskell extends Haskell with linear types, enabling resource-aware programming in a lazy functional language:

\begin{verbatim}
  -- Linear Haskell:
  consume :: a %1 -> ()     -- linear function: must use its argument
  duplicate :: a -> (a, a)  -- not linear: duplicates its argument

  -- Linear IO:
  readFile :: FilePath %1 -> IO (String, Handle)
  -- The file handle is returned and must be used linearly

  -- Linear arrays:
  writeArray :: MArray a %1 -> Int -> a -> MArray a
  -- The mutable array is consumed and returned (linear usage)
\end{verbatim}

Linear Haskell enables efficient in-place mutation of arrays and other data structures while maintaining referential transparency. The linearity ensures that the mutable array is used in a single-threaded manner.

