\subsection{Elaborating Conditional Destruction After a Move}

At scope exit, an affine local may still own its value along one
branch but have transferred it along another. A compiler cannot
emit an unconditional destructor at the join. One simple elaboration
uses a Boolean flag for each such local. Assume ownership checking
has already validated the program, the local has a non-throwing
destructor, and the compiler has identified every exit edge from its
scope. This sketch handles whole-value moves and initialization into
an empty local. It omits partial moves and overwrites, which need
more detailed storage state or a separate drop of the old value.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Affine local $x$; ownership events and scope exits
\State emit $f_x\gets\mathrm{false}$ at scope entry
\ForAll{successful initialization edges for $x$}
  \State emit $f_x\gets\mathrm{true}$ \Comment{Only after the value exists}
\EndFor
\ForAll{ownership transfer edges out of $x$}
  \State emit $f_x\gets\mathrm{false}$ at transfer \Comment{New owner drops}
\EndFor
\ForAll{edges leaving the scope of $x$}
  \State split edge with a cleanup block
  \State emit a branch testing $f_x$
  \State on the true arm, emit $f_x\gets\mathrm{false}$ then $\mathrm{drop}(x)$
  \State rejoin both arms and continue along the original edge
\EndFor
\end{sudocode}

For example, open file handle 17 as local \texttt{h}; successful
initialization sets the flag. When \texttt{send} is true, a call
consumes \texttt{h} and the transfer clears the flag at the
point at which the callee takes ownership. The join's cleanup then
skips handle 17. When \texttt{send} is false, the flag is still
set and the same cleanup closes handle 17 once. If opening fails
before initialization, the cleanup skips it on that path too.

At every point where cleanup can run, the flag is true exactly
when this local owns an initialized value. Placing the transfer event
on the wrong side of an exit or an unwind breaks that invariant.
When dataflow proves the flag constant at an exit, the branch can be
eliminated and the destructor emitted or omitted directly. With several
locals, cleanup also needs the language's destruction order; the flags
say which locals are present, not the order in which to destroy them.

\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-48}

Linear and affine type systems provide static guarantees about resource usage by tracking how values are consumed. Linear types require exact usage (once); affine types allow at-most-once usage with disposal. These disciplines, rooted in linear logic, find applications in file handle management, unique pointers, concurrent programming, and memory safety.

Rust's ownership system is the most prominent real-world application, demonstrating that affine types with borrowing can provide memory safety without garbage collection. The ongoing challenge is to make these systems more accessible to programmers while retaining their expressive power.

For further reading, we recommend Wadler's tutorial on linear types~\cite{wadler1990}, the Linear Haskell proposal~\cite{bernardy2017}, and the Rustonomicon's treatment of ownership and lifetimes.
